From: =?UTF-8?q?Max=20J=C3=A4ckle?=
 <141254171+jmax696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:48:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fast path covers parameter links with jnp.sqrt (amplitude
 modulation)

aligo(mode="amplitude_modulation") and voyager(mode="amplitude_modulation")
link a signal amplitude to sqrt(laser power). `fast.supports` rejected every
setup with links, so these public setups ran on the dense JAX path. Links
whose function is in `fast.LINK_RULES` (jnp.sqrt) are now evaluated on the
host: `EntryPlan._row` applies them to the parameter row after the design
variables and before the sweeps, as `simulate_jax` does, and
`EntryPlan.pullback` hands the cotangent of a linked slot to its source.
Any other linking function still uses the JAX path. Ported from Noel's
round twelve (b3030c4) to the current fast.py.

First run with the stages of benchmark.py, 101 frequencies, on an M4 (not
measured on x86 Linux): aLIGO amplitude modulation 5237 -> about 180-260 ms,
Voyager amplitude modulation 4696 -> about 200 ms.

Checked: benchmark.py and benchmark_aligo.py PASS (Mac, and Linux in Docker
with --cpus 1); all scripts under runs/noel, runs/philip and runs/flags pass;
runs/check_setups.py now expects the fast path for aLIGO amplitude
modulation; new runs/max/test_links.py compares outputs and gradients with
simulate_jax (worst gradient 9.1e-08, log sensitivity 1.1e-06 for Voyager at
the constructor's values, where the two detector ports cancel to 1e-5 of
one port). A second model (Codex) tried to break it with links without a
design variable, two links from one source, a swept source, other linking
functions, vmap and repeated calls, and found no mismatch.

Bytecode of fast.py and simulate.py regenerated.

(Source unchanged from origin/max/links 8877134. Applied on main 19132db, which had changed fast.py
in the meantime, with the bytecode of fast.py and simulate.py regenerated.)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
